==== `fix --json` reports each edit, what is left, and a summary
---- file a.azl
fn main() {
    let mut n = 1;
    let m = null
    print(n)
}
---- run fix --json --check a.azl
---- exit 1
---- stdout
{"fixed":{"code":"E0120","message":"`;` does not end a statement in azoL; the end of the line does","file":"a.azl","line":2,"column":18,"end_line":2,"end_column":19,"replacement":""}}
{"fixed":{"code":"E0121","message":"`let mut` is Rust; in azoL a variable that changes is declared with `var`","file":"a.azl","line":2,"column":5,"end_line":2,"end_column":12,"replacement":"var"}}
{"severity":"error","code":"E0222","message":"azoL has no `null`; a value that may be absent is an `Option[T]`, and absent is `None`","file":"a.azl","line":3,"column":13,"end_line":3,"end_column":17,"source":"    let m = null","rule":"There is no null: absence is `None` of an `Option[T]`. The truth values are `true` and `false`. There are no modules such as `Math`, `std` or `console`, and no `this`."}
{"summary":{"fixed":2,"errors":1,"files":1,"written":0,"held":1,"fixable":0}}
==== `fix` takes the files of one program together
---- file a.azl
fn main() {
    print(twice(2));
}
---- file b.azl
pub fn twice(n: int) -> int {
    return n * 2;
}
---- run fix a.azl b.azl
---- exit 0
---- stdout
fixed a.azl:2:20 E0120 `;` does not end a statement in azoL; the end of the line does
fixed b.azl:2:17 E0120 `;` does not end a statement in azoL; the end of the line does
fixed b.azl:1:1 E0126 azoL has no `pub`; every declaration is seen by the whole program
fixed b.azl:1:13 E0220 `int` is not an azoL type; the integer type is `i64`
fixed b.azl:1:21 E0220 `int` is not an azoL type; the integer type is `i64`
---- after a.azl
fn main() {
    print(twice(2))
}
---- after b.azl
fn twice(n: i64) -> i64 {
    return n * 2
}
